Context: Grothendieck is studying the 'équation des lacets' (the loop equation), apparently through explicit matrix solutions in rank 2. M is a rank-2 module over a base S and G = G(M) is a group scheme of automorphisms (GL or SL). The data are:
- sections ρ, σ;
- a unipotent one-parameter family ε_1(λ) = 1 + N_1 λ with N_1 nilpotent, and ε_0;
- unknowns: a triple (ξ, η, μ), where μ is called the 'multiplier'.

The unknowns satisfy equations (33), (34), (35). From neighbouring pages these appear to be: det ξ = 1, a trace condition, and η = ξ ε_1(1−μ) together with a trace condition on η. The structural hypothesis is (40 bis), which says ξ_1 = ε_1(1) is expressed through ρσ^{-1} with a scalar λ_0. Under it, the trace function Q(ξ) is the constant c = λ_0 Tr ρ + Tr σ, and one assumes (42) that c is everywhere invertible. The third equation then becomes P(ξ)μ = c.

This page states the resulting Proposition. Solutions (ξ, η, μ) of (33)–(35) correspond bijectively to sections ξ of G satisfying (45):
- det ξ = 1;
- Tr(ξρ) = Tr ρ;
- P(ξ) is an invertible section of O_S.
The inverse map is ξ ↦ (ξ, η = ξ ε_1(1−μ), μ = c/P(ξ)), and μ is automatically a unit.

In the NB he redoes the elimination with η as the unknown. One gets P(η)μ − Q'(η) = 0 with Q'(η) = Tr(η ε_0^{-1} ρ) − Tr ρ. The corresponding initial condition is ε_0^{-1} ρ = −λ'_0 σ, with λ'_0 a unit, equivalent to (40 bis) with λ'_0 = 1/λ_0. It makes Q' a constant c'. The next page shows c' = λ'_0 c, so the hypotheses are symmetric in ξ and η.

The next page then computes P(ξ) explicitly as a linear trace form −Tr(ξ N_1 σ), using ε_1 = 1 + N_1. So the solution set of the loop-type equation is identified with an explicit open subscheme (P ≠ 0) of a codimension-2 subscheme of SL_2 cut out by a trace condition. This is a concrete parametrisation of a moduli of matrix solutions, probably of rank-2 local-system / representation type.
